        "severity": "low",
        "category": "contract-violation",
        "title": "Base64 encoder/decoder lookups use ISO-8859-1 and lose characters above U+00FF",
        "reason": "The encoder and decoder both use ISO-8859-1 on purpose, so they round-trip any Latin-1 string and the base64 of an ASCII string is the same under either charset. Nothing in the Javadoc, the tests or the interpolator docs promises UTF-8 or a round trip for arbitrary Unicode. Switching to UTF-8 would change the encoded output for existing characters such as 'é', and decoding already-published base64 would change too, so a maintainer would be unlikely to accept it without a new opt-in charset. The '?' loss for '€' is a real limitation but a documented-by-code design choice, not a defect against a stated contract.",
